---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import ArticleUtilities from "../../../components/ArticleUtilities.astro";
import { formatInsightDate, insights } from "../../../data/insights";

const insight = insights.find((item) => item.slug === "pdpa-for-chros")!;
const title = "PDPA for CHROs | Nissanka Advisory";
const description = insight.summary;
const url = "https://nissanka.co.uk/insights/pdpa-for-chros/";
const questions = [
    { title: "Do we actually know what employee data we hold?", content: `<p>This sounds basic.</p><p>In large organisations, it rarely is.</p><p>Employee information may exist across HRIS platforms, payroll, recruitment systems, spreadsheets, learning platforms, benefits providers, occupational health systems, engagement tools, shared drives and local files.</p><p>Section 12 requires controllers to establish internal controls and maintain catalogued records demonstrating how data protection obligations are being implemented.</p><p>For HR, the starting point should therefore be a people-data inventory.</p><p>Not just systems.</p><p>Data.</p><ul><li>What do we collect?</li><li>Where is it?</li><li>Who can access it?</li><li>Why do we need it?</li><li>Who receives it?</li><li>How long do we retain it?</li></ul><p class="pull">You cannot govern information you cannot see.</p>` },
    { title: "Can we explain why we process each category of data?", content: `<p>The PDPA requires processing to have a lawful basis and requires personal data to be used for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes. It also introduces requirements around proportionality, accuracy and retention.</p><p>That creates a useful challenge for HR.</p><p>Just because information has historically been collected does not necessarily mean the organisation still needs it.</p><p>The question becomes:</p><blockquote><p>What decision or legitimate organisational purpose requires this data?</p></blockquote><p>That conversation can expose years of unnecessary collection and retention.</p>` },
    { title: "Are we treating sensitive HR information differently?", content: `<p>HR regularly handles information whose misuse could have significant consequences for an employee.</p><p>Health information is an obvious example.</p><p>Biometric attendance systems are another.</p><p>So are certain diversity records, disciplinary information and information relating to children or dependants.</p><p>The Act establishes additional conditions for processing special categories of personal data, including provisions specifically relevant to employment and social-security contexts.</p><p>A mature approach therefore cannot give every HR dataset the same controls.</p><p>The sensitivity of the information should influence access, security, retention and governance.</p>` },
    { title: "Do we know what our HR technology suppliers are doing with employee data?", content: `<p>This may become one of the biggest practical issues.</p><p>Most HR functions now rely on an ecosystem rather than one system.</p><ul><li>Payroll providers.</li><li>Recruitment platforms.</li><li>Cloud HR systems.</li><li>Background screening.</li><li>Learning platforms.</li><li>Benefits providers.</li><li>Employee listening tools.</li><li>AI applications.</li></ul><p>Under Part III, controllers using processors must use processors capable of providing appropriate technical and organisational measures and establish appropriate contractual arrangements covering the processing.</p><p>The 2025 amendment also replaced section 26 governing cross-border data flows. The amended provision requires controllers and processors engaging in cross-border data flows to meet specified protections, subject to the mechanisms and exceptions in the Act.</p><p>For many CHROs, one of the most useful exercises will simply be:</p><blockquote><p>Map every HR vendor that receives employee data and identify where that data is processed.</p></blockquote>` },
    { title: "Are we keeping employee information simply because we always have?", content: `<p>The PDPA requires personal data to be retained only for as long as necessary or required for the relevant purposes, subject to specified exceptions.</p><p>This can expose a common HR problem.</p><ul><li>Old applicant records.</li><li>Former employee records.</li><li>Historic performance documents.</li><li>Copies of passports.</li><li>Benefits information.</li><li>Spreadsheets created for projects years ago.</li></ul><p class="pull">Retention should become a deliberate decision rather than the absence of deletion.</p>` },
    { title: "What happens when HR starts using AI?", content: `<p>This is where data protection and the next generation of HR technology begin to collide.</p><p>The Act’s definition of profiling explicitly includes processing used to evaluate, analyse or predict someone’s performance at work, along with behaviour, health, preferences, economic situation and other characteristics.</p><p>The Act also provides for personal data protection impact assessments for certain forms of processing, including systematic and extensive evaluation or profiling and other processing identified as presenting relevant risks.</p><p>That means a new AI recruitment tool, talent-prediction model or employee monitoring application should not be viewed only as a technology purchase.</p><p>HR should be asking:</p><ul><li>What data is the AI using?</li><li>What is it inferring?</li><li>Who makes the final decision?</li><li>Can we explain the process?</li><li>What happens when the technology changes?</li></ul><p>The Act specifically provides for a fresh impact assessment where methodology, technology or process changes in processing that has already been assessed.</p><p>That is particularly relevant in an AI environment where systems change rapidly.</p>` },
    { title: "Who owns this inside the organisation?", content: `<p>PDPA readiness cannot belong to HR alone.</p><p>It also cannot simply be handed to Legal.</p><p>HR should be part of a governance model involving Legal, Information Security, Technology, Risk and business leadership.</p><p>Section 20 establishes circumstances in which a Data Protection Officer must be designated or appointed. The DPO role includes advising on data-processing requirements, supporting staff capability and providing advice on impact assessments. The 2025 amendment changed aspects of these provisions, including the DPO framework.</p><p>The better question for the CHRO is therefore:</p><blockquote><p>Who is accountable for people data, and how does HR connect into the organisation’s wider data-governance model?</p></blockquote>` },
];
const structuredData = { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [
    { "@type": "Article", "@id": `${url}#article`, headline: insight.title, alternativeHeadline: insight.subtitle, description, url, datePublished: insight.published, dateModified: insight.updated, author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://nissanka.co.uk/#gishan-nissanka", name: insight.author, url: "https://nissanka.co.uk/about/" }, publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Nissanka Advisory", url: "https://nissanka.co.uk/" }, about: [insight.topic, insight.geography, insight.theme], contentLocation: { "@type": "Country", name: "Sri Lanka" } },
    { "@type": "BreadcrumbList", itemListElement: [{ "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://nissanka.co.uk/" }, { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Insights", item: "https://nissanka.co.uk/insights/" }, { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: insight.title, item: url }] },
] };
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} path="/insights/pdpa-for-chros/" {structuredData}>
<article class="pdpa">
    <header class="hero"><div class="shell">
        <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Insights", href: "/insights/" }, { label: "PDPA for CHROs" }]} />
        <p class="system-label">{insight.geography} / {insight.topic} / {insight.format}</p>
        <div class="hero-grid"><div><h1>{insight.title}</h1><p class="subtitle">{insight.subtitle}</p></div><p class="standfirst">Few functions hold a broader range of personal information than HR. Sri Lanka’s Personal Data Protection Act therefore should not be treated as an IT or Legal issue alone.</p></div>
        <blockquote class="hero-question"><p>Do we understand the employee data we hold, why we hold it, where it goes and how it is governed?</p></blockquote>
        <dl class="meta"><div><dt>Topic</dt><dd>{insight.topic}</dd></div><div><dt>Geography</dt><dd>{insight.geography}</dd></div><div><dt>Format</dt><dd>{insight.format}</dd></div><div><dt>Published</dt><dd><time datetime={insight.published}>{formatInsightDate(insight.published)}</time></dd></div>{insight.updated && <div><dt>Updated</dt><dd><time datetime={insight.updated}>{formatInsightDate(insight.updated)}</time></dd></div>}</dl>
        <p class="author">By <a href="/about/">{insight.author}</a></p><ArticleUtilities title={insight.title} url="/insights/pdpa-for-chros/" />
    </div></header>

    <div class="reading prose opening"><p>For many organisations, data protection still sounds like an issue for Legal, IT or Information Security.</p><p>HR leaders should look at it differently.</p><p>Few functions hold a broader range of personal information than HR.</p><p>Think about what follows an employee through an organisation: a CV, identification details, salary, bank information, performance records, absence information, health data, benefits, learning history, succession assessments and sometimes biometric information.</p><p>Sri Lanka’s Personal Data Protection Act makes the governance of that information an organisational responsibility.</p><p>And 1 January 2027 is now an important date.</p><p>Extraordinary Gazette No. 2498/16, published on 22 July 2026, appoints that date for sections 2 and 3 and for Parts I and III of the PDPA to come into operation. Those provisions cover the scope of the law, core requirements for processing personal data, and obligations relating to controllers and processors.</p><p>For CHROs, the practical question is therefore not:</p><blockquote><p>“Is HR compliant with PDPA?”</p></blockquote><p>It is:</p><blockquote><p>“Do we understand the employee data we hold, why we hold it, where it goes and how it is governed?”</p></blockquote></div>

    <section class="section"><div class="shell split"><div><p class="system-label">The employee lifecycle</p><h2>HR holds some of the organisation’s most consequential data</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The Act defines personal data broadly. It includes information that can identify someone directly or indirectly, including identification numbers, financial information, location information and factors relating to physical, psychological, economic or social identity. Remuneration is specifically included within the definition of financial data.</p><p>Some HR information receives additional attention because it falls into special categories of personal data.</p><p>This includes health information, biometric data used for identification, racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, genetic information, information concerning sex life or sexual orientation, information relating to offences, and personal data relating to children.</p><p>That makes PDPA relevant across almost the entire employee lifecycle.</p></div></div><ol class="shell lifecycle"><li>Recruit</li><li>Employ</li><li>Develop</li><li>Reward</li><li>Monitor</li><li>Exit</li></ol><div class="reading prose emphasis"><p>The issue is not simply whether HR has a privacy policy.</p><p>The issue is what happens to the data at every stage.</p></div></section>

    <section class="section opportunity"><div class="shell"><p class="system-label">The opportunity</p><h2>This is a chance for HR to add strategic value, not simply respond to regulation.</h2><div class="prose intro"><p>Good governance does more than reduce risk.</p><p>It improves how organisations understand their people data.</p><p>Better governance can create clearer ownership, better data discipline and stronger data quality.</p><p>And better-quality people data creates the foundation for better workforce decisions.</p></div><ol class="value"><li>Good governance</li><li>Better data quality</li><li>Better workforce decisions</li></ol><p class="wide">For HR leaders, PDPA readiness can therefore become part of a wider conversation about the quality of workforce information, the decisions leaders can make from it and the organisation’s readiness for AI.</p></div></section>

    <figure class="shell infographic"><img src="/resources/sri-lanka/pdpa-for-chros-infographic.png" width="941" height="1672" loading="lazy" alt="PDPA for CHROs infographic showing five questions HR leaders should ask, the relationship between good governance, data quality and workforce decisions, and a 90-day starting plan." /><figcaption><span>PDPA for CHROs: a practical visual companion.</span><a href="/resources/sri-lanka/pdpa-for-chros-infographic.png" download>Download infographic →</a><a href="/resources/sri-lanka/pdpa-for-chros-infographic.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open full size →<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></figcaption></figure>

    <section class="section questions"><div class="shell"><p class="system-label">Seven questions</p><h2>Seven questions every CHRO should now ask</h2><div class="question-list">{questions.map((question, index) => <section class="question"><p class="number">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</p><div class="prose"><h3>{question.title}</h3><Fragment set:html={question.content} /></div></section>)}</div></div></section>

    <section class="section timing"><div class="shell split"><div><p class="system-label">Timing</p><h2>There is also an important timing point</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Organisations should avoid assuming that every provision of the PDPA becomes operational on 1 January 2027.</p><p>As at 2 October 2026, Gazette No. 2498/16 appoints that date for sections 2 and 3, Part I and Part III. Part II, which contains data-subject rights, is not included in that particular commencement order.</p><p>Organisations should therefore monitor further commencement orders, regulations, directives and guidance from the Data Protection Authority rather than relying on older implementation summaries.</p><p>That nuance does not reduce the need to prepare.</p><p>Part I and Part III already create substantial operational questions around how organisations process, govern, secure, document and share personal data.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="section agenda"><div class="shell"><p class="system-label">A practical starting point</p><h2>A practical 90-day CHRO agenda</h2><p class="wide">Rather than beginning with a large compliance programme, I would start with three moves.</p><ol><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Understand</h3><p>Map the employee-data lifecycle, HR systems, major datasets and third parties.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Prioritise</h3><p>Identify sensitive data, unclear purposes, excessive retention, high-risk processing, overseas processing and emerging AI use cases.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Act</h3><p>Agree ownership, update governance and contracts where required, establish appropriate controls and create a roadmap for the areas that cannot be resolved immediately.</p></div></li></ol><div class="reading prose"><p>The objective is not simply to create more policies.</p><p>It is to understand how people data actually moves through the organisation.</p><p>Because ultimately, good data protection is good data governance.</p><p>And for HR leaders, that is increasingly part of running a modern workforce.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="section closing"><div class="shell split"><h2>Good governance creates better data.<br />Better data creates better decisions.</h2><div><p>That is why PDPA should be viewed as more than a compliance deadline.</p><p>For HR, it is also an opportunity to improve the foundations on which workforce decisions are made.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="notice"><div class="reading"><p class="system-label">Important note</p><p>{insight.regulatory?.disclaimer}</p></div></section>
    {insight.references && <section class="section references"><div class="reading"><p class="system-label">Sources</p><h2>Sources &amp; References</h2><ol>{insight.references.map((reference) => <li><a href={reference.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{reference.label}<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></li>)}</ol></div></section>}
    <section class="section author-note"><div class="reading"><p class="system-label">Author</p><h2>Gishan Nissanka</h2><p>Gishan works across AI transformation, workforce intelligence, people technology and organisational decision-making.</p><a href="/about/">About Gishan →</a></div></section>
    <nav class="pathways" aria-label="Related pathways"><div class="shell"><p class="system-label">Continue exploring</p><a href="/sri-lanka/">Explore Sri Lanka →</a><a href="/advisory/ai-strategy/">AI Strategy &amp; Transformation →</a><a href="/insights/">Explore all Insights →</a></div></nav>
</article>
</BaseLayout>

